x^2 + y^2 > x^2 \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad y^2 > 0;
\] \[
x^2 + y^2 > y^2 \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad x^2 > 0;
\] \[
x^2 = x^2 + y^2 \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad y^2 = 0.
\] For $y^2 > 0$, we cannot be on the point $(x,0)$ for any value of $x$, so we ignore this case.  For $x^2 > 0$, we can be anywhere inside the unit circle except on the unit ball.  For $y^2 = 0$, we must be on the unit ball, but there cannot be a point inside the unit ball which is farther from $(1,0)$ than all of the points on the unit ball.  (Because, for any given distance $\epsilon$, we have the assumption that $(x,y)$ is inside the ball, thus $d((1,0),(x,y)) < \epsilon$.  If $(x',y')$ is inside the ball, then $d((1,0),(x,y)) \le d((1,0),(x’,y’)) \le \epsilon$.)

